\bottomrule
\multicolumn{6}{l}{\scriptsize $^\dagger$Grand mean across topologies. $^\ddagger$Negative (worsens bias). ---: see grand mean.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{DSL topology sensitivity.}
DSL corrects bias only where the misclassified variable enters the estimand as a confounder or mediator: confounding (49.8\% reduction) and mediation (70.3\% reduction). For the remaining five topologies, DSL increases bias, with M-bias exhibiting catastrophic amplification ($-$768\%). This occurs because DSL's gold-label reweighting is designed for the setting where $A^*$ is the primary variable of interest; when $A$ plays a different causal role (collider, instrument), the reweighting introduces new confounding rather than removing misclassification bias.

\paragraph{PPI++ caveats.}
PPI++ requires a gold-standard labeled subset, limiting its applicability when human annotation is expensive. Under realistic LLM error structures for M-bias, PPI++ shows negative bias reduction ($-$25.1\%), though coverage remains high (99\%). This suggests that even the best available correction method has topology-specific failure modes.

\paragraph{Practical implication.}
Correction method effectiveness is itself topology-dependent: no single method dominates across all causal designs. Practitioners must identify their DAG topology before selecting a correction strategy. For FWL-invariant topologies, only methods that modify the estimation procedure (PPI++, MC-SIMEX) can reduce bias; matrix-based corrections (naive, MLA) are provably ineffective on the target causal parameter.

